## Backward Compatibility

The UI API maintains 100% backward compatibility through a bridge system:

```rust
// Bridge to existing floating_bar commands
pub async fn bridge_to_floating_bar(&mut self, interaction: &UIInteractionEvent) -> Result<(), String> {
    match interaction.interaction_type.as_str() {
        "click" => crate::commands::floating_bar::floating_bar_click(self.app_handle.clone()).await?,
        "submit" => {
            if let Some(value) = interaction.data.get("value") {
                crate::commands::floating_bar::floating_bar_submit(
                    self.app_handle.clone(),
                    value.to_string()
                ).await?;
            }
        },
        // ... more interaction types
    }
}
```

## Development Rules

### 1. Use UI API for All New Components

**DO**: Create new floating UI elements using the standardized API

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
const ChatComponent = () => {
  const { manager, state, config } = useUIElement("main-chat", "chat");
  return <div>/* Chat UI */</div>;
};
```

**DON'T**: Create direct Tauri command calls for UI operations

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Don't use direct commands
const handleClick = () => {
  invoke("floating_bar_click"); // Old pattern
};
```

### 2. State Conversion Patterns

Always provide conversion utilities between component-specific states and UI API states:

```typescript
// ✅ REQUIRED - State conversion utilities
const convertBarStateToUIState = (barState: BarState): UIState => {
  switch (barState) {
    case "default": return "default";
    case "input": return "input";
    case "always-listening": return "always-listening";
    // ... complete mapping
  }
};

const convertUIStateToBarState = (uiState: UIState): BarState => {
  // Reverse mapping
};
```

### 3. Bidirectional State Synchronization

Components must sync state both ways between UI API and local component state:

```typescript
// ✅ REQUIRED - Sync UI API state to local state
useEffect(() => {
  if (state) {
    setLocalState(convertUIStateToLocalState(state.uiState));
    setInputValue(state.inputValue);
    // ... sync all relevant properties
  }
}, [state]);

// ✅ REQUIRED - Sync local changes back to UI API
const handleLocalChange = async (newValue: string) => {
  setInputValue(newValue);
  if (input) {
    await input(newValue);
  }
};
```

### 4. Type Safety Requirements

Always import and use proper types:

```typescript
// ✅ REQUIRED
import { useUIElement, UIState, AgentStatus, UIElementType } from "@/lib/ui-api";

// Type conversion functions must handle all cases
const convertToAgentStatus = (status: string): AgentStatus => {
  switch (status) {
    case "idle": return "idle";
    case "working": return "working";
    case "responding": return "responding";
    case "failed": return "failed";
    case "finished": return "finished";
    case "cancelled": return "cancelled";
    case "offline": return "offline";
    default: return "idle"; // Always provide default
  }
};
```

### 5. Hook Usage Patterns

Always destructure only the properties you need from useUIElement:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Only destructure needed properties
const { state, config, click, input, submit } = useUIElement("element-id", "bar");

// ❌ WRONG - Don't destructure unused properties
const {
  manager, state, config, click, input, submit, blur, focus,
  updateState, updateConfig
} = useUIElement("element-id", "bar"); // Creates unused variable errors
```

